                "content": "# Ideal-Gas Reaction Thermodynamics: A \u21cc B + C\n\n## Part (1): Explicit Expressions\n\n**Kirchhoff's Law** states $\\frac{d(\\Delta_r H^\\circ)}{dT} = \\Delta_r C_p^\\circ$. Since $\\Delta_r C_p^\\circ$ is constant, integration from $T_0$ to $T$ gives:\n\n$$\\boxed{\\Delta_r H^\\circ(T) = \\Delta_r H^\\circ(T_0) + \\Delta_r C_p^\\circ\\,(T - T_0)}$$\n\n**Thermodynamic entropy relation** $\\left(\\frac{\\partial S}{\\partial T}\\right)_p = C_p/T$ applied to the reaction:\n\n$$\\boxed{\\Delta_r S^\\circ(T) = \\Delta_r S^\\circ(T_0) + \\Delta_r C_p^\\circ \\ln\\frac{T}{T_0}}$$\n\n**Gibbs energy** from $\\Delta_r G^\\circ = \\Delta_r H^\\circ - T\\Delta_r S^\\circ$:\n\n$$\\boxed{\\Delta_r G^\\circ(T) = \\Delta_r H^\\circ(T_0) + \\Delta_r C_p^\\circ(T-T_0) - T\\,\\Delta_r S^\\circ(T_0) - T\\,\\Delta_r C_p^\\circ \\ln\\frac{T}{T_0}}$$\n\n**Equilibrium constant** from $\\Delta_r G^\\circ = -RT\\ln K_p$:\n\n$$\\boxed{\\ln K_p(T) = -\\frac{\\Delta_r H^\\circ(T_0) + \\Delta_r C_p^\\circ(T-T_0)}{RT} + \\frac{\\Delta_r S^\\circ(T_0)}{R} + \\frac{\\Delta_r C_p^\\circ}{R}\\ln\\frac{T}{T_0}}$$\n\n---\n\n## Part (2): Temperatures Where $K_p = 1$\n\nSetting $\\Delta_r G^\\circ(T) = 0$ gives $\\Delta_r H^\\circ(T) = T\\,\\Delta_r S^\\circ(T)$. Substituting the explicit forms:\n\n$$\\underbrace{\\bigl[15000 - 80(T-298.15)\\bigr]}_{\\Delta_r H^\\circ(T)} = T\\,\\underbrace{\\bigl[50 - 80\\ln(T/298.15)\\bigr]}_{\\Delta_r S^\\circ(T)}$$\n\nAfter collecting terms:\n\n$$38852 = 130\\,T - 80\\,T\\ln\\!\\frac{T}{298.15} \\tag{$\\star$}$$\n\nDefine $f(T) \\equiv 130T - 80T\\ln(T/298.15)$. Then $f'(T) = 50 - 80\\ln(T/298.15)$, which vanishes at $T^* = 298.15\\,e^{5/8} \\approx 557\\;\\text{K}$, a **maximum**. Since $f(T_0) = 38\\,759.5 < 38\\,852 < f(T^*) \\approx 44\\,559$, the equation $f(T) = 38\\,852$ has exactly **two** roots in $[200, 1000]\\;\\text{K}$.\n\n**First root** (on the rising branch, $T < T^*$):\n\n| $T$ (K) | $f(T)$ |\n|---------|--------|\n| 300.0 | 38 851.5 |\n| 300.1 | 38 856.5 |\n\nInterpolation yields $T_1 = 300.0\\;\\text{K}$.\n\n**Second root** (on the falling branch, $T > T^*$):\n\n| $T$ (K) | $f(T)$ |\n|---------|--------|\n| 861.8 | 38 853.1 |\n| 861.9 | 38 851.4 |\n\nInterpolation yields $T_2 = 861.9\\;\\text{K}$.\n\n$$\\boxed{T_1 = 300.0\\;\\text{K}, \\qquad T_2 = 861.9\\;\\text{K}}$$\n\n**Why two roots?** Although $\\Delta_r H^\\circ(298.15\\;\\text{K}) = +15\\;\\text{kJ/mol} > 0$, the **negative** $\\Delta_r C_p^\\circ = -80\\;\\text{J/(mol\u00b7K)}$ causes $\\Delta_r H^\\circ(T)$ to decrease linearly with $T$, crossing zero at $T = 38\\,852/80 = 485.7\\;\\text{K}$. Above this temperature the reaction becomes effectively **exothermic**. Simultaneously, $\\Delta_r S^\\circ(T)$ also decreases (becoming negative above $T^* \\approx 557\\;\\text{K}$). The competition between the linearly decreasing enthalpy and the logarithmically decreasing entropy, multiplied by $T$, produces a function $f(T)$ with a maximum \u2014 allowing the horizontal line $f = 38\\,852$ to be crossed twice.\n\n---\n\n## Part (3): $K_p$ at $T = 500.0\\;\\text{K}$\n\n$$\\Delta_r H^\\circ(500) = 15\\,000 - 80(500 - 298.15) = 15\\,000 - 16\\,148 = -1\\,148\\;\\text{J/mol}$$\n\n$$\\ln\\frac{500}{298.15} = \\ln(1.67701) = 0.51701$$\n\n$$\\Delta_r S^\\circ(500) = 50.00 - 80(0.51701) = 50.00 - 41.361 = 8.639\\;\\text{J/(mol\u00b7K)}$$\n\n$$\\Delta_r G^\\circ(500) = -1148 - 500(8.639) = -1148 - 4319.5 = -5467.5\\;\\text{J/mol}$$\n\n$$\\ln K_p = \\frac{5467.5}{8.3144626 \\times 500} = \\frac{5467.5}{4157.23} = 1.3152$$\n\n$$\\boxed{K_p(500\\;\\text{K}) = e^{1.3152} = 3.73}$$\n\n---\n\n## Part (4): Constant-Pressure System \u2014 Deriving $\\xi_P$\n\n**Initial state:** 1 mol A + 1 mol inert, no B or C, total pressure $p = 5.000\\;\\text{bar}$.\n\n**At equilibrium (extent $\\xi_P$):**\n\n| Species | Amount (mol) |\n|---------|-------------|\n| A | $1 - \\xi_P$ |\n| B | $\\xi_P$ |\n| C | $\\xi_P$ |\n| Inert | 1 |\n| **Total** | $2 + \\xi_P$ |\n\n**Mole fractions:**\n\n$$y_A = \\frac{1-\\xi_P}{2+\\xi_P}, \\quad y_B = y_C = \\frac{\\xi_P}{2+\\xi_P}$$\n\n**Reaction quotient** (dimensionless, with $p^\\circ = 1\\;\\text{bar}$):\n\n$$Q_p = \\frac{(y_B\\, p/p^\\circ)(y_C\\, p/p^\\circ)}{y_A\\, p/p^\\circ} = \\frac{y_B\\, y_C}{y_A}\\cdot\\frac{p}{p^\\circ} = \\frac{\\xi_P^2}{(1-\\xi_P)(2+\\xi_P)}\\cdot\\frac{5.000}{1.000}$$\n\n**Equilibrium condition** $Q_p = K_p = 3.726$ (using the unrounded value):\n\n$$\\frac{5\\xi_P^2}{(1-\\xi_P)(2+\\xi_P)} = 3.726$$\n\n$$5\\xi_P^2 = 3.726(1-\\xi_P)(2+\\xi_P) = 3.726(2 - 2\\xi_P + \\xi_P - \\xi_P^2) = 3.726(2 - \\xi_P - \\xi_P^2)$$\n\n$$5\\xi_P^2 = 7.452 - 3.726\\xi_P - 3.726\\xi_P^2$$\n\n$$\\boxed{8.726\\,\\xi_P^2 + 3.726\\,\\xi_P - 7.452 = 0}$$\n\nSolving with the quadratic formula ($\\xi_P > 0$ physically):\n\n$$\\xi_P = \\frac{-3.726 + \\sqrt{3.726^2 + 4(8.726)(7.452)}}{2(8.726)} = \\frac{-3.726 + \\sqrt{13.883 + 259.57}}{17.452}$$\n\n$$= \\frac{-3.726 + \\sqrt{273.45}}{17.452} = \\frac{-3.726 + 16.536}{17.452} = \\frac{12.810}{17.452}$$\n\n$$\\boxed{\\xi_P = 0.734}$$\n\n---\n\n## Part (5): Constant-Volume System \u2014 Deriving $\\xi_V$\n\n**Initial volume** (from ideal gas, initial $p_0 = 5.000\\;\\text{bar}$, $n_0 = 2$ mol):\n\n$$V = \\frac{n_0 RT}{p_0} = \\frac{2RT}{5.000}$$\n\n**Partial pressures at equilibrium** ($p_i = n_i RT/V$):\n\n$$p_A = \\frac{(1-\\xi_V)RT}{V} = \\frac{5(1-\\xi_V)}{2}\\;\\text{bar}$$\n\n$$p_B = p_C = \\frac{\\xi_V RT}{V} = \\frac{5\\xi_V}{2}\\;\\text{bar}$$\n\n**Reaction quotient:**\n\n$$Q_p = \\frac{(p_B/p^\\circ)(p_C/p^\\circ)}{p_A/p^\\circ} = \\frac{(5\\xi_V/2)^2}{5(1-\\xi_V)/2} = \\frac{25\\xi_V^2/4}{5(1-\\xi_V)/2} = \\frac{25\\xi_V^2/4 \\cdot 2}{5(1-\\xi_V)} = \\frac{5\\xi_V^2}{2(1-\\xi_V)}$$\n\n**Equilibrium condition** $Q_p = K_p = 3.726$:\n\n$$\\frac{5\\xi_V^2}{2(1-\\xi_V)} = 3.726$$\n\n$$5\\xi_V^2 = 7.452(1-\\xi_V) = 7.452 - 7.452\\,\\xi_V$$\n\n$$\\boxed{5\\,\\xi_V^2 + 7.452\\,\\xi_V - 7.452 = 0}$$\n\nSolving:\n\n$$\\xi_V = \\frac{-7.452 + \\sqrt{7.452^2 + 4(5)(7.452)}}{2(5)} = \\frac{-7.452 + \\sqrt{55.53 + 149.04}}{10}$$\n\n$$= \\frac{-7.452 + \\sqrt{204.57}}{10} = \\frac{-7.452 + 14.303}{10} = \\frac{6.851}{10}$$\n\n$$\\boxed{\\xi_V = 0.685}$$\n\n---\n\n## Part (6): Comparison and Explanation\n\n| | $\\xi_P$ (const. $p$) | $\\xi_V$ (const. $V$) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Value | 0.734 | 0.685 |\n| Total moles at eq. | $2 + 0.734 = 2.734$ | $2 + 0.685 = 2.685$ |\n| Total pressure at eq. | 5.000 bar (fixed) | $5\\times\\frac{2.685}{2} = 6.71$ bar (rises) |\n\n**$\\xi_P > \\xi_V$**: the constant-pressure system allows more conversion.\n\n### Le Chatelier's Principle\n\nThe reaction $\\text{A} \\rightleftharpoons \\text{B} + \\text{C}$ increases the number of moles ($\\Delta\\nu = +1$). Under **constant volume**, the production of additional moles raises the total pressure, which opposes the forward reaction (shifting equilibrium back toward A). Under **constant pressure**, the volume expands to accommodate the extra moles, so no such opposing pressure buildup occurs, and the forward reaction is less inhibited. Hence $\\xi_P > \\xi_V$.\n\n### Explicit Reaction-Quotient Analysis\n\nThe key difference lies in how the partial pressures enter $Q_p$:\n\n- **Constant $p$:** $Q_p = \\dfrac{\\xi_P^2}{(1-\\xi_P)(2+\\xi_P)}\\cdot\\dfrac{p}{p^\\circ}$\n\n  The denominator contains $(2+\\xi_P)$ from the total moles in the mole-fraction expression. As $\\xi$ grows, the factor $(2+\\xi)$ in the denominator **dilutes** all species, partially offsetting the growth of $\\xi^2$ in the numerator. This makes $Q_p$ smaller than it would be at constant volume for the same $\\xi$, so a **larger** $\\xi_P$ is needed to reach $K_p$.\n\n- **Constant $V$:** $Q_p = \\dfrac{5\\,\\xi_V^2}{2(1-\\xi_V)}$\n\n  There is no dilution factor. The numerator $\\xi^2$ grows quadratically while the denominator $(1-\\xi)$ shrinks linearly, so $Q_p$ reaches $K_p$ at a **smaller** extent.\n\nConcretely, at the same hypothetical extent $\\xi = 0.685$:\n\n$$Q_p^{(p)} = \\frac{5(0.685)^2}{(1-0.685)(2+0.685)} = \\frac{2.343}{(0.315)(2.685)} = \\frac{2.343}{0.846} = 2.77 < K_p$$\n\n$$Q_p^{(V)} = \\frac{5(0.685)^2}{2(1-0.685)} = \\frac{2.343}{0.630} = 3.72 = K_p \\checkmark$$\n\nSince $Q_p^{(p)} < K_p$ at $\\xi = 0.685$ under constant pressure, the system must proceed further (to $\\xi_P = 0.734$) before equilibrium is reached. This quantitatively confirms $\\xi_P > \\xi_V$.",
                "reasoning_content": "This is a comprehensive thermodynamics problem involving an ideal-gas reaction with temperature-dependent thermodynamic quantities. Let me work through each part carefully.\n\nGiven:\n- Reaction: A(g) \u21cc B(g) + C(g)\n- T\u2080 = 298.15 K\n- \u0394_r H\u00b0(T\u2080) = 15.00 kJ/mol = 15000 J/mol\n- \u0394_r S\u00b0(T\u2080) = 50.00 J/(mol\u00b7K)\n- \u0394_r C_p\u00b0 = -80.00 J/(mol\u00b7K) (constant over 200-1000 K)\n- p\u00b0 = 1.000 bar\n- R = 8.3144626 J/(mol\u00b7K)\n\n**Part (1): Derive explicit expressions**\n\nFrom Kirchhoff's law:\n$$\\frac{d(\\Delta_r H^\\circ)}{dT} = \\Delta_r C_p^\\circ$$\n\nSince \u0394_r C_p\u00b0 is constant:\n$$\\Delta_r H^\\circ(T) = \\Delta_r H^\\circ(T_0) + \\Delta_r C_p^\\circ (T - T_0)$$\n\nFor entropy, we use:\n$$\\frac{d(\\Delta_r S^\\circ)}{dT} = \\frac{\\Delta_r C_p^\\circ}{T}$$\n\nIntegrating:\n$$\\Delta_r S^\\circ(T) = \\Delta_r S^\\circ(T_0) + \\Delta_r C_p^\\circ \\ln\\frac{T}{T_0}$$\n\nFor Gibbs energy:\n$$\\Delta_r G^\\circ(T) = \\Delta_r H^\\circ(T) - T\\Delta_r S^\\circ(T)$$\n\nSubstituting:\n$$\\Delta_r G^\\circ(T) = \\Delta_r H^\\circ(T_0) + \\Delta_r C_p^\\circ(T - T_0) - T\\left[\\Delta_r S^\\circ(T_0) + \\Delta_r C_p^\\circ \\ln\\frac{T}{T_0}\\right]$$\n\nLet me expand this:\n$$\\Delta_r G^\\circ(T) = \\Delta_r H^\\circ(T_0) - T_0 \\Delta_r C_p^\\circ - T\\Delta_r S^\\circ(T_0) - T\\Delta_r C_p^\\circ \\ln\\frac{T}{T_0} + T\\Delta_r C_p^\\circ$$\n\nWait, let me be more careful:\n$$\\Delta_r G^\\circ(T) = \\Delta_r H^\\circ(T_0) + \\Delta_r C_p^\\circ T - \\Delta_r C_p^\\circ T_0 - T\\Delta_r S^\\circ(T_0) - T\\Delta_r C_p^\\circ \\ln\\frac{T}{T_0}$$\n\n$$= \\Delta_r H^\\circ(T_0) - T_0 \\Delta_r C_p^\\circ - T\\Delta_r S^\\circ(T_0) + T\\Delta_r C_p^\\circ - T\\Delta_r C_p^\\circ \\ln\\frac{T}{T_0}$$\n\n$$= [\\Delta_r H^\\circ(T_0) - T_0 \\Delta_r C_p^\\circ] - T\\Delta_r S^\\circ(T_0) + T\\Delta_r C_p^\\circ\\left(1 - \\ln\\frac{T}{T_0}\\right)$$\n\nFor the equilibrium constant:\n$$K_p(T) = \\exp\\left(-\\frac{\\Delta_r G^\\circ(T)}{RT}\\right)$$\n\nSo:\n$$\\ln K_p(T) = -\\frac{\\Delta_r G^\\circ(T)}{RT}$$\n\n$$= -\\frac{\\Delta_r H^\\circ(T_0) - T_0\\Delta_r C_p^\\circ}{RT} + \\frac{\\Delta_r S^\\circ(T_0)}{R} - \\frac{\\Delta_r C_p^\\circ}{R}\\left(1 - \\ln\\frac{T}{T_0}\\right)$$\n\nLet me verify: $\\frac{\\Delta_r G^\\circ(T)}{RT} = \\frac{\\Delta_r H^\\circ(T) - T\\Delta_r S^\\circ(T)}{RT} = \\frac{\\Delta_r H^\\circ(T)}{RT} - \\frac{\\Delta_r S^\\circ(T)}{R}$\n\n$$\\frac{\\Delta_r H^\\circ(T)}{RT} = \\frac{\\Delta_r H^\\circ(T_0) + \\Delta_r C_p^\\circ(T-T_0)}{RT}$$\n\n$$\\frac{\\Delta_r S^\\circ(T)}{R} = \\frac{\\Delta_r S^\\circ(T_0)}{R} + \\frac{\\Delta_r C_p^\\circ}{R}\\ln\\frac{T}{T_0}$$\n\nSo:\n$$\\ln K_p = -\\frac{\\Delta_r H^\\circ(T_0)}{RT} - \\frac{\\Delta_r C_p^\\circ(T-T_0)}{RT} - \\frac{\\Delta_r S^\\circ(T_0)}{R} - \\frac{\\Delta_r C_p^\\circ}{R}\\ln\\frac{T}{T_0}$$\n\n$$= -\\frac{\\Delta_r H^\\circ(T_0)}{RT} - \\frac{\\Delta_r C_p^\\circ T}{RT} + \\frac{\\Delta_r C_p^\\circ T_0}{RT} - \\frac{\\Delta_r S^\\circ(T_0)}{R} - \\frac{\\Delta_r C_p^\\circ}{R}\\ln\\frac{T}{T_0}$$\n\n$$= -\\frac{\\Delta_r H^\\circ(T_0)}{RT} + \\frac{\\Delta_r C_p^\\circ T_0}{RT} - \\frac{\\Delta_r C_p^\\circ}{R} - \\frac{\\Delta_r S^\\circ(T_0)}{R} - \\frac{\\Delta_r C_p^\\circ}{R}\\ln\\frac{T}{T_0}$$\n\n$$= -\\frac{\\Delta_r H^\\circ(T_0) - \\Delta_r C_p^\\circ T_0}{RT} - \\frac{\\Delta_r S^\\circ(T_0)}{R} - \\frac{\\Delta_r C_p^\\circ}{R}\\left(1 + \\ln\\frac{T}{T_0}\\right)$$\n\nWait, let me redo this more carefully.\n\n$$\\ln K_p(T) = -\\frac{\\Delta_r G^\\circ(T)}{RT}$$\n\n$$\\Delta_r G^\\circ(T) = \\Delta_r H^\\circ(T) - T\\Delta_r S^\\circ(T)$$\n\n$$\\Delta_r H^\\circ(T) = \\Delta_r H^\\circ(T_0) + \\Delta_r C_p^\\circ(T - T_0)$$\n\n$$\\Delta_r S^\\circ(T) = \\Delta_r S^\\circ(T_0) + \\Delta_r C_p^\\circ \\ln\\frac{T}{T_0}$$\n\nSo:\n$$\\Delta_r G^\\circ(T) = \\Delta_r H^\\circ(T_0) + \\Delta_r C_p^\\circ(T - T_0) - T\\Delta_r S^\\circ(T_0) - T\\Delta_r C_p^\\circ\\ln\\frac{T}{T_0}$$\n\n$$= \\Delta_r H^\\circ(T_0) - T_0\\Delta_r C_p^\\circ + \\Delta_r C_p^\\circ T - T\\Delta_r S^\\circ(T_0) - T\\Delta_r C_p^\\circ\\ln\\frac{T}{T_0}$$\n\n$$= [\\Delta_r H^\\circ(T_0) - T_0\\Delta_r C_p^\\circ] - T[\\Delta_r S^\\circ(T_0) - \\Delta_r C_p^\\circ + \\Delta_r C_p^\\circ\\ln\\frac{T}{T_0}]$$\n\nHmm, let me just keep it in the form:\n$$\\Delta_r G^\\circ(T) = \\Delta_r H^\\circ(T_0) + \\Delta_r C_p^\\circ(T-T_0) - T\\Delta_r S^\\circ(T_0) - T\\Delta_r C_p^\\circ\\ln\\frac{T}{T_0}$$\n\nAnd:\n$$\\ln K_p(T) = -\\frac{\\Delta_r H^\\circ(T_0) + \\Delta_r C_p^\\circ(T-T_0)}{RT} + \\frac{\\Delta_r S^\\circ(T_0)}{R} + \\frac{\\Delta_r C_p^\\circ}{R}\\ln\\frac{T}{T_0}$$\n\n**Part (2): Find temperatures where K_p = 1**\n\nK_p = 1 means \u0394_r G\u00b0(T) = 0, which means:\n$$\\Delta_r H^\\circ(T) = T\\Delta_r S^\\circ(T)$$\n\n$$\\Delta_r H^\\circ(T_0) + \\Delta_r C_p^\\circ(T-T_0) = T\\left[\\Delta_r S^\\circ(T_0) + \\Delta_r C_p^\\circ\\ln\\frac{T}{T_0}\\right]$$\n\nLet me plug in numbers:\n- \u0394_r H\u00b0(T\u2080) = 15000 J/mol\n- \u0394_r C_p\u00b0 = -80.00 J/(mol\u00b7K)\n- \u0394_r S\u00b0(T\u2080) = 50.00 J/(mol\u00b7K)\n- T\u2080 = 298.15 K\n\nSo:\n$$15000 - 80(T - 298.15) = T[50 - 80\\ln(T/298.15)]$$\n\n$$15000 - 80T + 80 \\times 298.15 = 50T - 80T\\ln(T/298.15)$$\n\n$$15000 - 80T + 23852 = 50T - 80T\\ln(T/298.15)$$\n\n$$38852 - 80T = 50T - 80T\\ln(T/298.15)$$\n\n$$38852 = 130T - 80T\\ln(T/298.15)$$\n\n$$38852 = T[130 - 80\\ln(T/298.15)]$$\n\nLet me define $f(T) = T[130 - 80\\ln(T/298.15)] - 38852 = 0$\n\nLet me check at various temperatures:\n\nAt T = 298.15: f = 298.15 \u00d7 [130 - 80\u00d70] - 38852 = 298.15 \u00d7 130 - 38852 = 38759.5 - 38852 = -92.5\n\nSo at T\u2080, f is slightly negative, meaning \u0394_r G\u00b0(T\u2080) > 0 (since we need T[130 - 80 ln(T/T\u2080)] to equal 38852, and at T\u2080 it gives 38759.5 < 38852).\n\nWait, let me double-check. At T = T\u2080:\n\u0394_r G\u00b0(T\u2080) = 15000 - 298.15 \u00d7 50 = 15000 - 14907.5 = 92.5 J/mol\n\nSo K_p(T\u2080) = exp(-92.5/(8.314 \u00d7 298.15)) = exp(-0.03736) \u2248 0.9633\n\nSo K_p < 1 at T\u2080. We need to find where K_p = 1.\n\nLet me try T = 300:\n\u0394_r H\u00b0(300) = 15000 - 80(300-298.15) = 15000 - 148 = 14852\n\u0394_r S\u00b0(300) = 50 + (-80)ln(300/298.15) = 50 - 80\u00d7ln(1.006205) = 50 - 80\u00d70.006186 = 50 - 0.4949 = 49.505\n\u0394_r G\u00b0(300) = 14852 - 300\u00d749.505 = 14852 - 14851.5 = 0.5\n\nHmm, that's very close to zero. Let me be more precise.\n\nln(300/298.15) = ln(1.006205...) \n\n300/298.15 = 1.006204...\nln(1.006204) \u2248 0.006185 (using ln(1+x) \u2248 x - x\u00b2/2 for small x)\n\nMore precisely: ln(1.006204) = 0.006204 - 0.006204\u00b2/2 + ... \u2248 0.006204 - 0.0000192 \u2248 0.006185\n\n\u0394_r S\u00b0(300) = 50 - 80(0.006185) = 50 - 0.4948 = 49.5052\n\u0394_r H\u00b0(300) = 15000 - 80(1.85) = 15000 - 148 = 14852\n\u0394_r G\u00b0(300) = 14852 - 300(49.5052) = 14852 - 14851.56 = 0.44 J/mol\n\nSo very close to zero at T = 300 K. Let me try to find it more precisely.\n\nLet me define g(T) = \u0394_r G\u00b0(T) = 15000 - 80(T-298.15) - T[50 - 80 ln(T/298.15)]\n\ng(T) = 15000 - 80T + 23852 - 50T + 80T ln(T/298.15)\n= 38852 - 130T + 80T ln(T/298.15)\n\nWe need g(T) = 0:\n38852 - 130T + 80T ln(T/298.15) = 0\n\nLet me try T = 299:\n80\u00d7299\u00d7ln(299/298.15) = 23920\u00d7ln(1.002851) = 23920\u00d70.002847 = 68.10\ng(299) = 38852 - 130\u00d7299 + 68.10 = 38852 - 38870 + 68.10 = 50.10\n\nT = 300:\n80\u00d7300\u00d7ln(300/298.15) = 24000\u00d70.006185 = 148.44\ng(300) = 38852 - 39000 + 148.44 = 0.44\n\nT = 300.5:\n80\u00d7300.5\u00d7ln(300.5/298.15) = 24040\u00d7ln(1.007883) = 24040\u00d70.007852 = 188.77\n\nWait, let me recalculate. 300.5/298.15 = 1.007883...\nln(1.007883) \u2248 0.007852 (approximately)\n80\u00d7300.5\u00d70.007852 = 24040\u00d70.007852 = 188.77\n\ng(300.5) = 38852 - 130\u00d7300.5 + 188.77 = 38852 - 39065 + 188.77 = -24.23\n\nSo the root is between 300 and 300.5. Let me try T = 300.1:\n300.1/298.15 = 1.006541\nln(1.006541) \u2248 0.006520\n80\u00d7300.1\u00d70.006520 = 24008\u00d70.006520 = 156.53\n\ng(300.1) = 38852 - 130\u00d7300.1 + 156.53 = 38852 - 39013 + 156.53 = -4.47\n\nT = 300.05:\n300.05/298.15 = 1.006373\nln(1.006373) \u2248 0.006353\n80\u00d7300.05\u00d70.006353 = 24004\u00d70.006353 = 152.49\n\ng(300.05) = 38852 - 130\u00d7300.05 + 152.49 = 38852 - 39006.5 + 152.49 = -2.01\n\nT = 300.02:\n300.02/298.15 = 1.006272\nln(1.006272) \u2248 0.006253\n80\u00d7300.02\u00d70.006253 = 24001.6\u00d70.006253 = 150.08\n\ng(300.02) = 38852 - 130\u00d7300.02 + 150.08 = 38852 - 39002.6 + 150.08 = -0.52\n\nT = 300.01:\n300.01/298.15 = 1.006239\nln(1.006239) \u2248 0.006220\n80\u00d7300.01\u00d70.006220 = 24000.8\u00d70.006220 = 149.29\n\ng(300.01) = 38852 - 130\u00d7300.01 + 149.29 = 38852 - 39001.3 + 149.29 = 0.0 (approximately)\n\nLet me be more precise. Let me use the equation:\n38852 = 130T - 80T ln(T/298.15)\n\nAt T = 300.01:\n130\u00d7300.01 = 39001.3\nT/298.15 = 300.01/298.15 = 1.0062384...\nln(1.0062384) = let me compute more carefully\nln(1+x) where x = 0.0062384\n= x - x\u00b2/2 + x\u00b3/3 - ...\n= 0.0062384 - 0.00001947 + ... \u2248 0.0062190\n\n80\u00d7300.01\u00d70.0062190 = 24000.8 \u00d7 0.0062190 = 149.26\n\nRHS = 39001.3 - 149.26 = 38852.04\n\nLHS = 38852\n\nSo at T = 300.01, RHS \u2248 LHS. The root is approximately T \u2248 300.0 K.\n\nWait, let me be more precise. Let me try T = 300.00:\n130\u00d7300 = 39000\n300/298.15 = 1.0062050...\nln(1.0062050) = 0.0062050 - 0.0062050\u00b2/2 = 0.0062050 - 0.00001924 = 0.0061858\n\n80\u00d7300\u00d70.0061858 = 24000\u00d70.0061858 = 148.46\n\nRHS = 39000 - 148.46 = 38851.54\nLHS = 38852\n\nSo g(300) = 38852 - 38851.54 = 0.46 > 0\n\nAt T = 300.01:\n130\u00d7300.01 = 39001.3\n300.01/298.15 = 1.0062384\nln(1.0062384) = 0.0062384 - 0.00001944 = 0.0062190\n\n80\u00d7300.01\u00d70.0062190 = 24000.8\u00d70.0062190 = 149.26\n\nRHS = 39001.3 - 149.26 = 38852.04\nLHS = 38852\n\ng(300.01) = 38852 - 38852.04 = -0.04\n\nSo the root is between 300.00 and 300.01. By linear interpolation:\nT\u2081 \u2248 300.00 + 0.46/(0.46+0.04) \u00d7 0.01 = 300.00 + 0.92\u00d70.01 = 300.009\n\nSo T\u2081 \u2248 300.0 K (to 0.1 K precision).\n\nNow let me check for another root at higher temperatures.\n\nLet me check the behavior of g(T) = 38852 - 130T + 80T ln(T/298.15)\n\nAs T \u2192 \u221e, the dominant term is -130T + 80T ln(T/298.15) = T[80 ln(T/298.15) - 130]\n\nSince ln(T/298.15) grows without bound, eventually 80 ln(T/298.15) > 130, i.e., ln(T/298.15) > 1.625, i.e., T/298.15 > e^1.625 \u2248 5.08, i.e., T > 1515 K.\n\nBut we're only going up to 1000 K. Let me check at T = 1000:\n130\u00d71000 = 130000\n1000/298.15 = 3.3540\nln(3.3540) = 1.2102\n80\u00d71000\u00d71.2102 = 96816\n\ng(1000) = 38852 - 130000 + 96816 = 5668 > 0\n\nAt T = 500:\n130\u00d7500 = 65000\n500/298.15 = 1.6770\nln(1.6770) = 0.5171\n80\u00d7500\u00d70.5171 = 20684\n\ng(500) = 38852 - 65000 + 20684 = -5464 < 0\n\nAt T = 700:\n130\u00d7700 = 91000\n700/298.15 = 2.3478\nln(2.3478) = 0.8535\n80\u00d7700\u00d70.8535 = 47796\n\ng(700) = 38852 - 91000 + 47796 = -4352 < 0\n\nAt T = 800:\n130\u00d7800 = 104000\n800/298.15 = 2.6832\nln(2.6832) = 0.9870\n80\u00d7800\u00d70.9870 = 63168\n\ng(800) = 38852 - 104000 + 63168 = -1980 < 0\n\nAt T = 900:\n130\u00d7900 = 117000\n900/298.15 = 3.0186\nln(3.0186) = 1.1048\n80\u00d7900\u00d71.1048 = 79546\n\ng(900) = 38852 - 117000 + 79546 = 1398 > 0\n\nSo there's a root between 800 and 900.\n\nAt T = 850:\n130\u00d7850 = 110500\n850/298.15 = 2.8509\nln(2.8509) = 1.0476\n80\u00d7850\u00d71.0476 = 71438\n\ng(850) = 38852 - 110500 + 71438 = -210 < 0\n\nAt T = 860:\n130\u00d7860 = 111800\n860/298.15 = 2.8844\nln(2.8844) = 1.0594\n80\u00d7860\u00d71.0594 = 72846\n\ng(860) = 38852 - 111800 + 72846 = -102 < 0\n\nAt T = 870:\n130\u00d7870 = 113100\n870/298.15 = 2.9180\nln(2.9180) = 1.0710\n80\u00d7870\u00d71.0710 = 74638\n\ng(870) = 38852 - 113100 + 74638 = 390 > 0\n\nSo root between 860 and 870.\n\nAt T = 862:\n130\u00d7862 = 112060\n862/298.15 = 2.8912\nln(2.8912) = 1.0617\n80\u00d7862\u00d71.0617 = 73053\n\ng(862) = 38852 - 112060 + 73053 = -155 < 0\n\nHmm, let me recalculate more carefully.\n\nAt T = 865:\n130\u00d7865 = 112450\n865/298.15 = 2.9012\nln(2.9012) = ?\n\nln(2.9) = ln(2.9). Let me compute: ln(3) = 1.0986, ln(2.9) = ln(3) + ln(2.9/3) = 1.0986 + ln(0.9667) = 1.0986 - 0.0339 = 1.0647\n\nActually, let me use: ln(2.9012) = ln(2.9) + ln(2.9012/2.9) = 1.0647 + ln(1.000414) \u2248 1.0647 + 0.000414 = 1.0651\n\n80\u00d7865\u00d71.0651 = 69200\u00d71.0651 = 73705\n\ng(865) = 38852 - 112450 + 73705 = 107 > 0\n\nAt T = 863:\n130\u00d7863 = 112190\n863/298.15 = 2.8946\nln(2.8946) \u2248 ln(2.9) + ln(2.8946/2.9) = 1.0647 + ln(0.99814) \u2248 1.0647 - 0.00186 = 1.0628\n\n80\u00d7863\u00d71.0628 = 69040\u00d71.0628 = 73376\n\ng(863) = 38852 - 112190 + 73376 = 38 > 0\n\nAt T = 862:\n130\u00d7862 = 112060\n862/298.15 = 2.8912\nln(2.8912) \u2248 1.0647 + ln(2.8912/2.9) = 1.0647 + ln(0.99697) \u2248 1.0647 - 0.00303 = 1.0617\n\n80\u00d7862\u00d71.0617 = 68960\u00d71.0617 = 73215\n\ng(862) = 38852 - 112060 + 73215 = -9 < 0\n\nAt T = 862.5:\n130\u00d7862.5 = 112125\n862.5/298.15 = 2.8928\nln(2.8928) \u2248 1.0647 + ln(2.8928/2.9) = 1.0647 + ln(0.99752) \u2248 1.0647 - 0.00248 = 1.0622\n\n80\u00d7862.5\u00d71.0622 = 69000\u00d71.0622 = 73292\n\ng(862.5) = 38852 - 112125 + 73292 = 19 > 0\n\nAt T = 862.2:\n130\u00d7862.2 = 112086\n862.2/298.15 = 2.8919\nln(2.8919) \u2248 1.0647 + ln(2.8919/2.9) = 1.0647 + ln(0.99721) \u2248 1.0647 - 0.00279 = 1.0619\n\n80\u00d7862.2\u00d71.0619 = 68976\u00d71.0619 = 73246\n\ng(862.2) = 38852 - 112086 + 73246 = 12 > 0\n\nAt T = 862.1:\n130\u00d7862.1 = 112073\n862.1/298.15 = 2.8916\nln(2.8916) \u2248 1.0647 + ln(2.8916/2.9) = 1.0647 + ln(0.99710) \u2248 1.0647 - 0.00290 = 1.0618\n\n80\u00d7862.1\u00d71.0618 = 68968\u00d71.0618 = 73238\n\ng(862.1) = 38852 - 112073 + 73238 = 17 > 0\n\nHmm, I'm getting inconsistent results. Let me redo this more carefully.\n\nActually, let me reconsider my approach. Let me define:\nh(T) = 38852 - 130T + 80T ln(T/298.15)\n\nAnd find zeros of h(T).\n\nLet me use a more systematic approach. Let x = T/298.15, so T = 298.15x.\n\nh(T) = 38852 - 130(298.15x) + 80(298.15x)ln(x)\n= 38852 - 38759.5x + 23852x\u00b7ln(x)\n= 38852 + x[23852 ln(x) - 38759.5]\n\nWe need: 38852 + x[23852 ln(x) - 38759.5] = 0\nx[23852 ln(x) - 38759.5] = -38852\nx[38759.5 - 23852 ln(x)] = 38852\n\nLet me define F(x) = x[38759.5 - 23852 ln(x)] and find where F(x) = 38852.\n\nAt x = 1 (T = 298.15): F(1) = 1\u00d7[38759.5 - 0] = 38759.5 < 38852\n\nAt x = 300/298.15 = 1.00620: F(1.00620) = 1.00620\u00d7[38759.5 - 23852\u00d70.006185] = 1.00620\u00d7[38759.5 - 147.54] = 1.00620\u00d738612.0 = 38851.0 \u2248 38852\n\nSo x\u2081 \u2248 1.0062, giving T\u2081 \u2248 300.0 K.\n\nFor the second root, I need x > 1 such that F(x) = 38852 again.\n\nF(x) = x[38759.5 - 23852 ln(x)]\n\nAs x increases, 38759.5 - 23852 ln(x) decreases. F(x) will eventually decrease (when 38759.5 - 23852 ln(x) < 0, i.e., ln(x) > 38759.5/23852 = 1.6251, i.e., x > e^1.6251 \u2248 5.08).\n\nBut before that, F(x) might have a maximum. Let me find the maximum of F(x).\n\nF'(x) = [38759.5 - 23852 ln(x)] + x\u00d7(-23852/x) = 38759.5 - 23852 ln(x) - 23852 = 14907.5 - 23852 ln(x)\n\nSetting F'(x) = 0: 23852 ln(x) = 14907.5, ln(x) = 0.6250, x = e^0.6250 = 1.8682\n\nSo F has a maximum at x = 1.8682, corresponding to T = 298.15 \u00d7 1.8682 = 557.0 K.\n\nF(1.8682) = 1.8682\u00d7[38759.5 - 23852\u00d70.6250] = 1.8682\u00d7[38759.5 - 14907.5] = 1.8682\u00d723852 = 44559\n\nSo F(x) increases from 38759.5 at x=1 to a maximum of 44559 at x=1.8682, then decreases.\n\nSince F(1) = 38759.5 < 38852 < 44559 = F_max, there are two solutions for F(x) = 38852:\n1. One with 1 < x < 1.8682 (which we found: x\u2081 \u2248 1.0062, T\u2081 \u2248 300.0 K)\n2. One with x > 1.8682\n\nFor the second root, I need to find x > 1.8682 where F(x) = 38852.\n\nAt x = 2.85 (T \u2248 849.7):\nF(2.85) = 2.85\u00d7[38759.5 - 23852\u00d7ln(2.85)]\nln(2.85) = ln(2) + ln(1.425) = 0.6931 + 0.3544 = 1.0475\nF(2.85) = 2.85\u00d7[38759.5 - 23852\u00d71.0475] = 2.85\u00d7[38759.5 - 25000.3] = 2.85\u00d713759.2 = 39214\n\nThat's > 38852.\n\nAt x = 2.9 (T \u2248 864.6):\nln(2.9) = 1.0647\nF(2.9) = 2.9\u00d7[38759.5 - 23852\u00d71.0647] = 2.9\u00d7[38759.5 - 25395.7] = 2.9\u00d713363.8 = 38755\n\nThat's < 38852.\n\nSo the second root is between x = 2.85 and x = 2.9.\n\nAt x = 2.87 (T = 298.15\u00d72.87 = 855.7):\nln(2.87) = ln(2.85) + ln(2.87/2.85) = 1.0475 + ln(1.00702) = 1.0475 + 0.00699 = 1.0545\nF(2.87) = 2.87\u00d7[38759.5 - 23852\u00d71.0545] = 2.87\u00d7[38759.5 - 25152.3] = 2.87\u00d713607.2 = 39053\n\n> 38852\n\nAt x = 2.89 (T = 298.15\u00d72.89 = 861.6):\nln(2.89) = ln(2.87) + ln(2.89/2.87) = 1.0545 + ln(1.00697) = 1.0545 + 0.00695 = 1.0614\nF(2.89) = 2.89\u00d7[38759.5 - 23852\u00d71.0614] = 2.89\u00d7[38759.5 - 25316.6] = 2.89\u00d713442.9 = 38850\n\n\u2248 38852! Very close.\n\nLet me check more carefully.\nx = 2.89:\nln(2.89): Let me compute this more precisely.\nln(2.89) = ln(289/100) = ln(289) - ln(100)\nln(289) = ln(17\u00b2) = 2ln(17) = 2\u00d72.8332 = 5.6664\nln(100) = 4.6052\nln(2.89) = 5.6664 - 4.6052 = 1.0612\n\nF(2.89) = 2.89\u00d7[38759.5 - 23852\u00d71.0612] = 2.89\u00d7[38759.5 - 25311.8] = 2.89\u00d713447.7 = 38864\n\nHmm, that's slightly above 38852.\n\nLet me try x = 2.895:\nln(2.895) = ln(2.89) + ln(2.895/2.89) = 1.0612 + ln(1.001730) = 1.0612 + 0.001728 = 1.0629\nF(2.895) = 2.895\u00d7[38759.5 - 23852\u00d71.0629] = 2.895\u00d7[38759.5 - 25352.7] = 2.895\u00d713406.8 = 38814\n\n< 38852\n\nSo root is between x = 2.89 and x = 2.895.\n\nAt x = 2.892:\nln(2.892) \u2248 1.0612 + 0.2\u00d70.001728 = 1.0612 + 0.000346 = 1.0616\nF(2.892) = 2.892\u00d7[38759.5 - 23852\u00d71.0616] = 2.892\u00d7[38759.5 - 25321.6] = 2.892\u00d713437.9 = 38863\n\nHmm, still above. 