.8\n\ng(862) = 38852 - 112060 + 73212.8 = 4.8\n\nOK so g(861) \u2248 -3.1 and g(862) \u2248 4.8.\n\nLinear interpolation: T\u2082 \u2248 861 + 3.1/(3.1+4.8) = 861 + 3.1/7.9 = 861 + 0.39 = 861.4\n\nLet me check at T = 861.4:\nT/298.15 = 2.89 + (861.4-861.65)/298.15 = 2.89 - 0.25/298.15 = 2.89 - 0.000839 = 2.889161\n\nWait, that's wrong. 861.4 < 861.65, so 861.4/298.15 < 2.89.\n\n861.4/298.15: We know 298.15 \u00d7 2.89 = 861.65. So 861.4/298.15 = 2.89 - 0.25/298.15 = 2.89 - 0.000839 = 2.889161\n\nln(2.889161) = ln(2.89) + ln(2.889161/2.89) = 1.06126 + ln(1 - 0.25/861.65) = 1.06126 + ln(1 - 0.000290)\n\u2248 1.06126 - 0.000290 = 1.06097\n\n80T ln(T/298.15) = 80 \u00d7 861.4 \u00d7 1.06097 = 68912 \u00d7 1.06097\n= 68912 + 68912 \u00d7 0.06097\n= 68912 + 68912 \u00d7 0.06 + 68912 \u00d7 0.00097\n= 68912 + 4134.7 + 66.8\n= 73113.5\n\ng(861.4) = 38852 - 130\u00d7861.4 + 73113.5 = 38852 - 111982 + 73113.5 = -16.5\n\nHmm, that's more negative than g(861). That can't be right if the function is increasing.\n\nWait, I think I made an error. Let me recheck.\n\ng(861) = 38852 - 111930 + 73047.9 = -3.1 \u2713\ng(861.4) = 38852 - 111982 + 73113.5 = -16.5\n\nBut g(862) = 38852 - 112060 + 73212.8 = 4.8\n\nThat's inconsistent. g(861.4) should be between g(861) and g(862).\n\nLet me recheck g(861.4):\n-130 \u00d7 861.4 = -111982\n80 \u00d7 861.4 = 68912\n68912 \u00d7 1.06097 = ?\n\nLet me redo: 68912 \u00d7 1.06 = 68912 + 68912 \u00d7 0.06 = 68912 + 4134.72 = 73046.72\n68912 \u00d7 0.00097 = 66.84\nTotal: 73046.72 + 66.84 = 73113.56\n\ng(861.4) = 38852 - 111982 + 73113.56 = -16.44\n\nAnd g(861) = 38852 - 111930 + 73047.9 = -3.1\n\nSo g(861.4) < g(861)? That means the function is decreasing at T = 861?\n\nLet me check g'(T) = -130 + 80 ln(T/298.15) + 80 = -50 + 80 ln(T/298.15)\n\nAt T = 861: g'(861) = -50 + 80 \u00d7 1.06051 = -50 + 84.84 = 34.84 > 0\n\nSo the function should be increasing. But I'm getting g(861.4) < g(861). I must have an arithmetic error.\n\nLet me recheck g(861):\n80 \u00d7 861 = 68880\n68880 \u00d7 1.06051:\n68880 \u00d7 1 = 68880\n68880 \u00d7 0.06 = 4132.8\n68880 \u00d7 0.0005 = 34.44\n68880 \u00d7 0.00001 = 0.6888\n68880 \u00d7 0.0000005 = 0.034\nTotal = 68880 + 4132.8 + 34.44 + 0.6888 + 0.034 = 73047.96\n\ng(861) = 38852 - 111930 + 73047.96 = -3.04\n\nNow g(861.4):\n80 \u00d7 861.4 = 68912\n68912 \u00d7 1.06097:\n68912 \u00d7 1 = 68912\n68912 \u00d7 0.06 = 4134.72\n68912 \u00d7 0.0009 = 62.02\n68912 \u00d7 0.00007 = 4.82\n68912 \u00d7 0.0000009 = 0.062\nTotal = 68912 + 4134.72 + 62.02 + 4.82 + 0.062 = 73113.62\n\ng(861.4) = 38852 - 111982 + 73113.62 = -16.38\n\nHmm, so g(861) = -3.04 and g(861.4) = -16.38. That means the function decreased. But g' > 0 at both points. Something is wrong.\n\nOh wait, I think I see the issue. Let me recheck ln(2.889161).\n\n2.889161/2.89 = 1 - 0.000839/2.89... no wait.\n\n2.89 - 2.889161 = 0.000839\n2.889161/2.89 = 1 - 0.000839/2.89 = 1 - 0.000290\n\nln(1 - 0.000290) \u2248 -0.000290\n\nln(2.889161) = 1.06126 - 0.000290 = 1.06097\n\nThat seems right.\n\nHmm, let me try a different approach. Let me compute g(T) directly at several points.\n\nActually, I wonder if I'm making an error in the sign. Let me go back to the original equation.\n\n\u0394_r G\u00b0(T) = 0 means:\n\u0394_r H\u00b0(T) = T \u0394_r S\u00b0(T)\n\n\u0394_r H\u00b0(T) = 15000 + (-80)(T - 298.15) = 15000 - 80T + 23852 = 38852 - 80T\n\n\u0394_r S\u00b0(T) = 50 + (-80)ln(T/298.15) = 50 - 80ln(T/298.15)\n\nSo the condition is:\n38852 - 80T = T(50 - 80ln(T/298.15))\n38852 - 80T = 50T - 80T ln(T/298.15)\n38852 = 130T - 80T ln(T/298.15)\n38852 = T(130 - 80ln(T/298.15))\n\nLet me define f(T) = T(130 - 80ln(T/298.15)) and find where f(T) = 38852.\n\nf(T) = 130T - 80T ln(T/298.15)\n\nf'(T) = 130 - 80ln(T/298.15) - 80 = 50 - 80ln(T/298.15)\n\nAt T = 861: f'(861) = 50 - 80\u00d71.06051 = 50 - 84.84 = -34.84 < 0\n\nSo f is DECREASING at T = 861! That makes sense because we're past the maximum of f (which occurs at 50 = 80ln(T/298.15), i.e., ln(T/298.15) = 0.625, T/298.15 = e^0.625 = 1.868, T = 557 K).\n\nSo for T > 557 K, f(T) is decreasing. Since f(T) starts at 38852 near T = 300 K (first root), increases to a maximum, then decreases, and we need f(T) = 38852 again, the second root is where f(T) decreases back to 38852.\n\nLet me recompute:\nf(861) = 130\u00d7861 - 80\u00d7861\u00d7ln(861/298.15) = 111930 - 68880\u00d71.06051 = 111930 - 73047.96 = 38882.04\n\nf(862) = 130\u00d7862 - 80\u00d7862\u00d7ln(862/298.15) = 112060 - 68960\u00d71.06167 = 112060 - 73212.8 = 38847.2\n\nSo f(861) = 38882 and f(862) = 38847. We need f(T) = 38852.\n\nLinear interpolation: T\u2082 \u2248 861 + (38882-38852)/(38882-38847) = 861 + 30/35 = 861 + 0.86 = 861.86\n\nLet me verify at T = 861.9:\nT/298.15 = 861.9/298.15\n298.15 \u00d7 2.89 = 861.65\n861.9 - 861.65 = 0.25\n0.25/298.15 = 0.000839\nT/298.15 = 2.890839\n\nln(2.890839) = 1.06126 + ln(2.890839/2.89) = 1.06126 + ln(1 + 0.25/861.65) = 1.06126 + 0.000290 = 1.06155\n\nf(861.9) = 130\u00d7861.9 - 80\u00d7861.9\u00d71.06155 = 112047 - 68952\u00d71.06155\n= 112047 - 68952 - 68952\u00d70.06155\n= 112047 - 68952 - 4243.6\n= 38851.4\n\nSo f(861.9) \u2248 38851.4, which is very close to 38852.\n\nLet me try T = 861.85:\nT/298.15 = 2.89 + 0.2/298.15 = 2.89 + 0.000671 = 2.890671\nln(2.890671) = 1.06126 + 0.000671/2.89 = 1.06126 + 0.000232 = 1.06149\n\nf(861.85) = 130\u00d7861.85 - 80\u00d7861.85\u00d71.06149 = 112040.5 - 68948\u00d71.06149\n= 112040.5 - 68948 - 68948\u00d70.06149\n= 112040.5 - 68948 - 4239.9\n= 38852.6\n\nSo f(861.85) \u2248 38852.6 and f(861.9) \u2248 38851.4.\n\nThe root is between 861.85 and 861.9.\n\nLinear interpolation: T\u2082 \u2248 861.85 + (38852.6-38852)/(38852.6-38851.4) \u00d7 0.05 = 861.85 + 0.6/1.2 \u00d7 0.05 = 861.85 + 0.025 = 861.875\n\nSo T\u2082 \u2248 861.9 K (to 0.1 K precision).\n\nHmm, but let me double-check. The problem says \"Give each temperature to 0.1 K.\" So I need to determine whether it's 861.8 or 861.9.\n\nT\u2082 \u2248